<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herzog August Library</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1572">1572</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Herzog August Bibliothek</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Herzog August Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, was founded in 1572 by Duke Julius of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel and greatly enlarged by Duke Augustus the Younger, after whom it is named. By the late 17th century it was one of the largest libraries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthold-ephraim-lessing">Gotthold Ephraim Lessing</a> served as its librarians, and it remains a major research library.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herzog-august-library.jpg" alt="Herzog August Library" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brunswyk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wolfenbuettel_Herzog_August_Bibliothek_Innen2_(2006).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Duke Julius began collecting books around 1550 while studying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. From 1558 he bought theological writings, and in 1567 his first large collection, the library of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> councillor Michael von Kaden, rich in legal and humanist works. Between 1570 and 1572, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> was introduced in the duchy, the libraries of the monasteries of Dorstadt, Wöltingerode, Heiningen and Steterburg were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>.</p><p>His library ordinance of 5 April 1572 counts as the founding document, although the church musician Leonhart Schröter, regarded as the first librarian, had been in charge since 1571. In 1578 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> from the estate of the theologian Johannes Aurifaber were added.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Duke Heinrich Julius, who inherited the library in 1589, added the estate of the theologian Matthias Flacius and the collections of further monasteries. In 1618, however, Duke Friedrich Ulrich handed the whole collection, by then about 5,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and prints, to the library of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-helmstedt">University of Helmstedt</a>. Large parts came back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> only in 1810, after the university closed.</p><p>The present library grew from the collection of Augustus the Younger, who acted as his own librarian from 1604 until his death in 1666. Armies passed back and forth over the centuries, but generals placed the library under special protection, and it is one of the oldest libraries never to have lost part of its collection. Later librarians included Paul Raabe, from 1968 to 1992. The Lower Saxony Ministry for Science and Culture oversees it today.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 2006 the library held about 11,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and 900,000 books, of which 350,000 were printed between the 15th and 18th centuries, among them 3,500 incunabula. Its manuscripts include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, the Liber Floridus of about 1150, nine volumes from the library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a>, the manuscripts W1 and W2 of the Magnus liber organi, the palimpsest Codex Guelferbytanus 64 Weissenburgensis and Luther's Wolfenbüttel Psalter, the only surviving copy of his glosses on his lectures on the Psalms from 1513. Hundreds of scholars from abroad work there on projects in the history of culture, religion, law, science and gender from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> to the early modern period.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herzog August Library” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Augustus the Younger built the <b>Herzog August Library</b>, one of the great libraries of Europe, where Lessing later served as librarian.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-wolfenbuttel">Principality of Wolfenbüttel</a></li></ul></section>
